# Line endings and encoding
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This repository is edited from Windows and deployed on Linux. Without this
# file, a file written by a Windows tool can enter the repository with CRLF
# endings, and PowerShell's `>` and `>>` operators write UTF-16LE by default —
# which is how README.md acquired a run of null bytes and started being treated
# as a binary file by git.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises everything to LF in the repository while
# leaving the working copy alone. Every tracked file is already LF, so adding
# this changes nothing today; it only prevents the next accident.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, because a stray CRLF in these is actively harmful rather than
# merely untidy: shell scripts fail with "bad interpreter", and SQL run through
# some clients breaks on the carriage return.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf

# Never normalise these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf  binary
